window.docContents[221].resolve({"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Lists-of-Numbers--Type-Classes-and-Overloading--Head-and-Tail":{"contents":"\n\ndef head (default : Nat) (l : NatList) : Nat :=\n  match l with\n  | [] => default\n  | h :: _ => h\n\n\nBasic theorems about how head behaves:\n\ntheorem head_cons {h x : Nat} {t : NatList} : (h :: t).head x = h := by rfl\n\ntheorem head_nil {x : Nat} : [].head x = x := by rfl\n\ndef tail (l : NatList) : NatList :=\n  match l with\n  | [] => []\n  | _ :: t => t\n\n\nBasic theorems about how tail behaves:\n\ntheorem tail_cons {h : Nat} {t : NatList} : (h :: t).tail = t := by rfl\n\ntheorem tail_nil : [].tail = [] := by rfl\n\n\n\n\nWhat does the following function do?def foo (n : Nat) : NatList :=\n  match n with\n  | 0 => []\n  | n' + 1 => (n' + 1) :: foo n'\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Lists: Working with Structured Data\u0009Lists of Numbers\u0009Type Classes and Overloading","header":"5.3.3.1. Head and Tail","id":"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Lists-of-Numbers--Type-Classes-and-Overloading--Head-and-Tail"}});